// produced by the code as it stood when this file was written; test/linkApprovalPins.test.js checks
// that it still produces each one.
//
// The chat output policy also removes a name written without a scheme (free-nitro.com/claim, bit.ly/abc,
// EVIL.COM: bareDomains in src/platform/linkPolicy.js). The rows after "names written without a scheme"
// were recorded with that rule, and six earlier rows changed with it: awww.example.com, a bare
// github.com page of an unapproved release, and four bare invites. Those six are marked below.
//
// Two more changes came after that rule: a name whose label in front of the ending is made of Chinese,
// Japanese or Korean letters alone now needs a port, path, query or fragment to be a link (the row for
// 領獎中心.com, which was removed and now stays), and an https:// address whose first character is not ASCII
// is removed whole. The rows for them stand in the sections "a name of Chinese letters" and "an address
// whose first character is not ASCII" at the end of the names.
//
// The last rows are of the rounds after that: an address whose first character is not ASCII needs a host
// in its run (so 網址要以https://開頭 is text), and an approved address wrapped in Markdown emphasis is
// kept, with the markers outside it. Whether the run is a host is decided by the parser a browser opens it
// with (an IP address in any spelling, or a name of two labels or more, whose last label is an ending: ASCII,
// the name of a top-level domain written in other letters, or "xn--" as written; a run the parser refuses for
// an invisible character is read without it too). The ideographic full stop is a dot, but a sentence behind
// it is no ending: https://開頭。這是常識 and a paragraph of several sentences are text, as they were, and
// 請輸入以https://開頭的網址。請注意大小寫 is the row it was, unchanged. The rows of those rounds stand in
// the sections "an address whose first character is not ASCII" and after it.
//
